5.2 Fusion Module

\hat{m} = \mathrm{Fuse}(\hat{m}_C, \hat{m}_D, c_C, c_D)

Proposition 5.1 (Fusion Safety Condition). Chemical recall  \hat{m}_C  is accepted only if:

\|F_t - F_{t'}\| < \epsilon \quad \text{and} \quad c_C > \tau_c

Otherwise, the system falls back to  \hat{m}_D .

Drift-compensated fingerprinting (new in v0.7). A fixed  \epsilon -threshold will false-reject healthy memories under nonstationarity. Replace with:

F_t = \Phi(z_t - \hat{d}_t)

where  \hat{d}_t  is a slow drift estimator learned from empty-well control runs. Alternatively, use a calibrated confidence model  c_C(y)  instead of a hard threshold.

Concrete implementations to be ablated:

· Concatenation:  \hat{m} = [\hat{m}_C; \hat{m}_D] , followed by a linear projection.
· Attention:  \hat{m} = \mathrm{Attention}(q_D, K_C, V_C) .
· Gated fusion:  \hat{m} = g \odot \hat{m}_C + (1-g) \odot \hat{m}_D , where  g = \sigma(W[c_C; c_D]) .
· Digital verification: as in Proposition 5.1.

5.3 Encoder/Decoder

Encoder  E_\phi: \mathcal{M} \times \mathcal{Q} \to \mathcal{U}^T :

u_{t_0:t_0+T} = E_\phi(m_t, q_t, x_{t_0})

For the formose CSTR testbed:

·  m_t \in \mathbb{R}^{d_m} : continuous memory vector.
·  u_t \in \mathbb{R}^5 : five input flow rates (formaldehyde, dihydroxyacetone, sodium hydroxide, calcium chloride, water).

