7. Safety and Control Constraint

The LLM must not directly control the physical chemical substrate. The proposed action is:

a_t = C_{\mathrm{safe}}(a_t^{LLM}, x_t)

where  C_{\mathrm{safe}}  enforces hard physical constraints. For Phase III (in chemico),  C_{\mathrm{safe}}  must include physical interlocks — flow limiters, hard valve cutoffs, temperature/pressure interlocks, emergency stop, operator override — in addition to software constraints. A learned/software function is not a hard constraint on real hardware; it can fail out-of-distribution.

EHS specifics (new in v0.7).

· BZ: bromate (oxidizer, carcinogen suspect), malonic acid (irritant), cerium/ferroin catalyst. Fume hood required; bromate waste stream segregated.
· Formose: formaldehyde (carcinogen, Class 1B), NaOH (corrosive), CaCl₂ (irritant). Formaldehyde scrubber required; closed-loop flow preferred.
· DNA: aqueous, low hazard, but oligonucleotide waste disposal per institutional biohazard policy.

EHS review is required before any wet-lab step.

Formal specification. The safety controller solves:

C_{\mathrm{safe}}(a, x) = \arg\min_{a' \in \mathcal{A}} \|a' - a\|^2 \quad \text{s.t.} \quad g_i(a', x) \leq 0

where  g_i  encode concentration bounds, temperature limits, pressure limits, and toxicity constraints.

