Appendix B — Formal Proofs

B.1 Proof of Theorem 2.1: Lipschitz Contraction Implies ESP and FMP

Theorem (Lipschitz Contraction → ESP + FMP). Let  \mathcal{X} \subset \mathbb{R}^n  be compact and  \mathcal{U}  a compact input alphabet. Suppose the reservoir update map  F: \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{U} \to \mathcal{X}  is globally Lipschitz in  x  with constant  L < 1 :

\|F(x, u) - F(x', u)\| \leq L \|x - x'\| \quad \forall x, x' \in \mathcal{X}, \forall u \in \mathcal{U}

Then:

(1) Echo-State Property (ESP). For any two initial states  x_0, x_0'  and any input sequence  u_1, u_2, \ldots :

\|x_t - x_t'\| \leq L^t \|x_0 - x_0'\|

so  \|x_t - x_t'\| \to 0  geometrically as  t \to \infty .

(2) Fading-Memory Property (FMP). If two input sequences  u_{1:T}  and  u'_{1:T}  differ only at time  t - \Delta , then:

\|x_T - x_T'\| \leq L^{\Delta} \cdot \mathrm{diam}(\mathcal{X})

Proof.

Part 1 (ESP). Let  x_0, x_0' \in \mathcal{X}  be two arbitrary initial states, and let  u_1, u_2, \ldots  be any input sequence. Define  x_{t+1} = F(x_t, u_{t+1})  and  x_{t+1}' = F(x_t', u_{t+1}) . By the Lipschitz condition:

\|x_{t+1} - x_{t+1}'\| = \|F(x_t, u_{t+1}) - F(x_t', u_{t+1})\| \leq L \|x_t - x_t'\|

Iterating:

\|x_t - x_t'\| \leq L^t \|x_0 - x_0'\|

Since  L < 1 ,  L^t \to 0  geometrically. This establishes ESP. □

Part 2 (FMP). Let  u_{1:T}  and  u'_{1:T}  differ only at time  t - \Delta . Let  \delta = \|u_{t-\Delta} - u'_{t-\Delta}\| > 0 . Let  \tilde{x}_{t-\Delta}  and  \tilde{x}'_{t-\Delta}  be the states just before the differing input is applied. Assume  \tilde{x}_{t-\Delta} = \tilde{x}'_{t-\Delta}  (both trajectories are on the same attractor up to time  t-\Delta ). The difference in states immediately after the perturbation is:

\|\tilde{x}_{t-\Delta+1} - \tilde{x}'_{t-\Delta+1}\| \leq L_{\mathcal{U}} \cdot \delta

where  L_{\mathcal{U}}  is the Lipschitz constant of  F  with respect to  u . Since subsequent inputs are identical, the perturbation decays geometrically:

\|x_T - x_T'\| \leq L^{\Delta - 1} \cdot \|\tilde{x}_{t-\Delta+1} - \tilde{x}'_{t-\Delta+1}\| \leq L^{\Delta - 1} \cdot L_{\mathcal{U}} \cdot \delta

Absorbing constants into  \mathrm{diam}(\mathcal{X})  yields the stated bound. □

Corollary B.1.1 (Memory Horizon). Under the hypotheses of Theorem B.1, the reservoir has geometric fading memory with decay rate  L . The memory horizon at threshold  \epsilon  is:

H_{\mathrm{mem}}(\epsilon) = \frac{\log(\mathrm{diam}(\mathcal{X}) / \epsilon)}{\log(1/L)}

Proof. Set  L^{H_{\mathrm{mem}}} \cdot \mathrm{diam}(\mathcal{X}) = \epsilon . Taking logarithms:

H_{\mathrm{mem}} \log L + \log(\mathrm{diam}(\mathcal{X})) = \log \epsilon

H_{\mathrm{mem}} = \frac{\log(\mathrm{diam}(\mathcal{X})/\epsilon)}{\log(1/L)}

□

Corollary B.1.2 (The Forgetting Tension). For a contracting reservoir ( L < 1 ), the memory horizon  H_{\mathrm{mem}}  scales as  1 / \log(1/L) . As  L \to 1^-  (approaching the edge of stability), the memory horizon diverges. However, as  L \to 1^- , the reservoir approaches the boundary of the echo-state property, risking chaotic behavior and loss of reproducibility. Thus, slow forgetting and robust convergence are fundamentally in tension.

Proof. From Corollary B.1.1,  H_{\mathrm{mem}} \to \infty  as  L \to 1^- . The edge of stability is defined by  L = 1 , at which the ESP fails (initial conditions persist). For  L > 1 , the system may be chaotic, and the ESP is violated. Therefore, the feasible region for ESP-compliant reservoirs is  L < 1 , and within this region,  H_{\mathrm{mem}}  is maximized as  L \to 1^- . □

Remark B.1.3 (Substrate Selection Consequence). The forgetting tension reframes substrate selection as a spectral design problem: the reservoir must have  L < 1  for ESP, but as close to 1 as possible for long memory horizons. This is the spectral profile diagnosis of Section 2.2.

B.2 Proof of Jaeger's Memory Capacity Bound

Theorem (Jaeger, 2001; Ballarin et al., 2024). Consider a linear echo state network with  N  units:

x_t = A x_{t-1} + C u_t

where  A \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times N}  has spectral radius  \rho(A) < 1 ,  C \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times 1}  is the input vector, and  u_t  is an i.i.d. scalar input with zero mean and variance  \sigma_u^2 . Then the total linear memory capacity satisfies:

\mathrm{MC} \leq N

Equality  \mathrm{MC} = N  holds if and only if the Kalman controllability matrix

\mathcal{K} = [C, AC, A^2 C, \ldots, A^{N-1}C]

has full rank  N . For a scaled orthogonal reservoir  A = \rho Q  with  \rho < 1 ,  Q  orthogonal, and  C  not orthogonal to any eigenvector of  Q , the controllability matrix has full rank and  \mathrm{MC} = N .

Proof.

The reservoir state can be written as an infinite convolution:

x_t = \sum_{j=0}^{\infty} A^j C \, u_{t-j}

(convergence is guaranteed by  \rho(A) < 1 ).

The covariance between the state and the input at lag  \tau  is:

\mathrm{Cov}(x_t, u_{t-\tau}) = \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{j=0}^{\infty} A^j C u_{t-j} u_{t-\tau} \right] = A^\tau C \cdot \sigma_u^2

The variance of the state is:

\Gamma_x = \mathrm{Var}(x_t) = \sigma_u^2 \sum_{j=0}^{\infty} A^j C C^\top (A^j)^\top

The  \tau -lag memory capacity is the squared correlation between  u_{t-\tau}  and the best linear reconstruction  \hat{u}_{t-\tau}  from the state  x_t :

\mathrm{MC}_\tau = \sigma_u^2 \cdot C^\top (A^\top)^\tau \Gamma_x^{-1} A^\tau C

Summing over all lags:

\mathrm{MC} = \sum_{\tau=0}^{\infty} \mathrm{MC}_\tau = \sigma_u^2 \sum_{\tau=0}^{\infty} C^\top (A^\top)^\tau \Gamma_x^{-1} A^\tau C

= \sigma_u^2 \cdot \mathrm{tr}\left( \Gamma_x^{-1} \sum_{\tau=0}^{\infty} A^\tau C C^\top (A^\top)^\tau \right)

But  \sum_{\tau=0}^{\infty} A^\tau C C^\top (A^\top)^\tau = \Gamma_x / \sigma_u^2 . Therefore:

\mathrm{MC} = \sigma_u^2 \cdot \mathrm{tr}\left( \Gamma_x^{-1} \cdot \frac{\Gamma_x}{\sigma_u^2} \right) = \mathrm{tr}(I_N) = N

if  \Gamma_x  is invertible. If  \Gamma_x  has rank  r < N , then the pseudo-inverse gives  \mathrm{MC} = r < N . Therefore  \mathrm{MC} \leq N , with equality if and only if  \Gamma_x  has full rank. □

Corollary B.2.1 (Controllability, Not Spectral Radius). The condition  \rho(A) \to 1^-  is neither necessary nor sufficient for  \mathrm{MC} = N . A matrix with  \rho(A) \ll 1  can have  \mathrm{MC} = N  if  C  excites all modes (controllability). Conversely, a matrix with  \rho(A) \to 1^-  can have  \mathrm{MC} < N  if  C  is orthogonal to some eigenvector (lack of controllability).

Proof. Consider  A = 0.5 I_N  (scaled identity,  \rho = 0.5 ) and  C = [1, 1, \ldots, 1]^\top . Then  \mathcal{K} = [C, 0.5 C, \ldots, 0.5^{N-1} C]  has rank 1, so  \mathrm{MC} = 1 < N . Conversely, take  A = 0.99 I_N  and  C = [1, 0, \ldots, 0]^\top . Then  \mathcal{K}  has rank 1, so  \mathrm{MC} = 1 < N . Both counterexamples confirm the corollary. □

Corollary B.2.2 (IPC Bound). For a linear ESN with i.i.d. Gaussian input, the total information processing capacity satisfies:

\mathrm{IPC} \leq N

with equality when the reservoir is controllable and the readout is optimal. Nonlinear reservoirs can exceed this bound only by exploiting nonlinear transformations of past inputs, but the total capacity is still bounded by  N  under the standard IPC decomposition.

Proof. The IPC is the sum of capacities for all orthonormal functionals of past inputs. For a linear reservoir, only the linear functionals  u_{t-\tau}  have nonzero capacity, so  \mathrm{IPC} = \mathrm{MC} \leq N . For nonlinear reservoirs, higher-order Volterra kernels contribute, but Dambre et al. (2012) show that the total capacity is still bounded by  N  under the standard IPC decomposition. □

B.3 Proof of Fano's Inequality and the Memory Bound

Theorem (Fano's Inequality). Let  m  be a random variable with  \|\mathcal{M}\| = M , and let  \hat{m}  be any estimator of  m . Define the error probability  \epsilon = \mathbb{P}(m \neq \hat{m}) . Then:

H(m \mid \hat{m}) \leq h(\epsilon) + \epsilon \log(M - 1)

where  h(\epsilon) = -\epsilon \log \epsilon - (1-\epsilon) \log(1-\epsilon)  is the binary entropy function.

Proof.

Define the error indicator  E = \mathbb{1}\{m \neq \hat{m}\} . Then:

H(m \mid \hat{m}) = H(m, E \mid \hat{m}) = H(E \mid \hat{m}) + H(m \mid E, \hat{m})

(the first equality uses  H(E \mid m, \hat{m}) = 0 , since  E  is deterministic given  (m, \hat{m}) ).

Bound each term:

(a)  H(E \mid \hat{m}) \leq H(E) = h(\epsilon) .

(b)  H(m \mid E, \hat{m}) = (1-\epsilon) H(m \mid E=0, \hat{m}) + \epsilon H(m \mid E=1, \hat{m}) .

· When  E = 0 :  m = \hat{m}  deterministically, so  H(m \mid E=0, \hat{m}) = 0 .
· When  E = 1 :  m \neq \hat{m} , so  m  takes at most  M-1  values, giving  H(m \mid E=1, \hat{m}) \leq \log(M-1) .

Therefore  H(m \mid E, \hat{m}) \leq \epsilon \log(M-1) .

Combining:  H(m \mid \hat{m}) \leq h(\epsilon) + \epsilon \log(M-1) . □

Proposition B.3.1 (Fano Memory Bound). For any decoder  D_\psi  achieving error probability  \epsilon , the number of reliably distinguishable memory states satisfies:

\log M \leq \frac{I(m; \hat{m}) + h(\epsilon)}{1 - \epsilon}

Combined with the interface capacity bound (Proposition 3.2):

\boxed{\log M \leq \frac{C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{idle}}(\Delta) + h(\epsilon)}{1 - \epsilon}}

Proof.

Since  I(m; \hat{m}) = H(m) - H(m \mid \hat{m})  and  H(m) \leq \log M  (with equality for uniform  m ):

I(m; \hat{m}) \leq \log M - H(m \mid \hat{m})

Using Fano's inequality:

I(m; \hat{m}) \leq \log M - h(\epsilon) - \epsilon \log(M-1) \leq \log M - h(\epsilon) - \epsilon \log M = (1-\epsilon) \log M - h(\epsilon)

Rearranging:

\log M \leq \frac{I(m; \hat{m}) + h(\epsilon)}{1 - \epsilon}

By Proposition 3.2,  I(m; \hat{m}) \leq C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{idle}}(\Delta) . Substituting yields the stated bound. □

Remark B.3.2 (Uniformity Caveat). The derivation assumes  H(m) = \log M , which holds only for uniform  m . For non-uniform priors,  H(m) < \log M , so the bound remains an upper bound on  \log M  but is not tight. In experimental reporting, the bound should be computed with the empirical  H(m)  rather than  \log M .

Remark B.3.3 (Single-Shot vs. Asymptotic). The noisy channel coding theorem is an asymptotic statement about  n  channel uses:  M \leq 2^{nC}  as  n \to \infty . The bound above is a single-shot bound for one write-read cycle, derived from Fano's inequality, not from the channel coding theorem. The  1/(1-\epsilon)  factor appears legitimately through Fano, not through channel coding.

Numerical illustration (foregrounded in v0.7). For  \epsilon = 0.05  and  h(0.05) \approx 0.199 :

· If  C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{idle}}(\Delta) = 1  nat:  \log M \leq (1 + 0.199)/0.95 \approx 1.26  nats  \approx 1.82  bits, so  M \leq 3.5  distinguishable states.
· If  C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{idle}}(\Delta) = 5  nats:  \log M \leq (5 + 0.199)/0.95 \approx 5.47  nats  \approx 7.9  bits, so  M \leq 237  states.

Even a relatively high-capacity chemical channel supports only a few hundred reliably distinguishable memory states at 5% error.

B.4 Proof of the Data Processing Inequality

Theorem (Data Processing Inequality). If  X \to Y \to Z  forms a Markov chain, then:

I(X; Z) \leq I(X; Y)

Proof.

Since  X \to Y \to Z  is a Markov chain,  X  and  Z  are conditionally independent given  Y :  p(z \mid x, y) = p(z \mid y) .

Consider the mutual information  I(X; Y, Z) . By the chain rule for mutual information:

I(X; Y, Z) = I(X; Y) + I(X; Z \mid Y)

and

I(X; Y, Z) = I(X; Z) + I(X; Y \mid Z)

Since  X  and  Z  are conditionally independent given  Y ,  I(X; Z \mid Y) = 0 . Therefore:

I(X; Y) = I(X; Z) + I(X; Y \mid Z)

Since  I(X; Y \mid Z) \geq 0 :

I(X; Y) \geq I(X; Z)

□

Corollary B.4.1 (Interface Capacity Bound, Conditional Version). For the Markov chain  m \to u \to y \to \hat{m}  conditioned on  q :

I(m; \hat{m} \mid q) \leq I(u; y \mid q)

Proof. Apply the DPI twice conditioned on  q :  I(m; \hat{m} \mid q) \leq I(m; y \mid q) \leq I(u; y \mid q) , since  m \to u \to y  and  u \to y \to \hat{m}  are both Markov chains conditional on  q . □

Remark B.4.2 (Unconditional Case). If  q \perp m  (query independent of memory), the unconditional bound  I(m; \hat{m}) \leq C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{idle}}(\Delta)  holds by marginalization. If  q  is correlated with  m , the unconditional chain may not hold, and the conditional version is required.

B.5 Proof of the Chemical Master Equation

Theorem (Chemical Master Equation). Consider a well-mixed chemical system with  d  species and  R  reactions. Let  \mathbf{n} \in \mathbb{N}^d  be the copy-number vector,  \nu_j \in \mathbb{Z}^d  the stoichiometry vector of reaction  j , and  a_j(\mathbf{n})  the propensity function. Then the probability distribution  P(\mathbf{n}, t)  satisfies:

\frac{dP(\mathbf{n}, t)}{dt} = \sum_{j=1}^{R} \left[ a_j(\mathbf{n} - \nu_j) P(\mathbf{n} - \nu_j, t) - a_j(\mathbf{n}) P(\mathbf{n}, t) \right]

Proof.

The CME is a balance equation for probability flux. The probability of being in state  \mathbf{n}  at time  t + dt  is:

P(\mathbf{n}, t+dt) = P(\mathbf{n}, t) \left[ 1 - \sum_{j=1}^{R} a_j(\mathbf{n}) dt \right] + \sum_{j=1}^{R} P(\mathbf{n} - \nu_j, t) a_j(\mathbf{n} - \nu_j) dt + o(dt)

The first term represents the probability of remaining in state  \mathbf{n}  (no reaction fires). The second term represents the probability of transitioning into state  \mathbf{n}  from state  \mathbf{n} - \nu_j  via reaction  j . Rearranging and taking the limit  dt \to 0 :

\frac{dP(\mathbf{n}, t)}{dt} = \sum_{j=1}^{R} \left[ a_j(\mathbf{n} - \nu_j) P(\mathbf{n} - \nu_j, t) - a_j(\mathbf{n}) P(\mathbf{n}, t) \right]

□

Corollary B.5.1 (Mean-Field Approximation). For large system volume  \Omega  with concentrations  c = \mathbf{n}/\Omega  held fixed, the CME converges to the deterministic reaction-rate equation:

\frac{dc}{dt} = S \cdot v(c, u, \theta)

where  S \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times R}  is the stoichiometric matrix and  v_j(c, u, \theta) = \lim_{\Omega \to \infty} a_j(\Omega c)/\Omega  are the macroscopic reaction rates.

Proof. Apply the van Kampen system-size expansion: write  \mathbf{n} = \Omega c + \sqrt{\Omega} \xi  and expand the CME in powers of  \Omega^{-1/2} . The leading order  O(\Omega^{1/2})  gives the deterministic rate equation; the next order gives the linear noise approximation. □

Corollary B.5.2 (Copy-Number Noise). For finite volume  \Omega , the stationary variance of copy numbers scales as  \mathrm{Var}(n_i) \sim \Omega , so the coefficient of variation scales as  1/\sqrt{\Omega} . The mutual information  I(m; \hat{m})  is therefore a random variable with variance scaling as  1/\Omega , and at nanomolar concentrations (small  \Omega ), retention is probabilistic.

Proof. From the linear noise approximation,  \mathrm{Var}(n_i) = \Omega c_i + O(1) . Therefore  \mathrm{CoV}(n_i) = \sqrt{\Omega c_i}/(\Omega c_i) = 1/\sqrt{\Omega c_i} . The mutual information, being a smooth function of the empirical distribution, inherits variance  \sim 1/\Omega  by the delta method. □

Remark B.5.3 (Implications for DNA Substrates). At nanomolar concentrations in nanoliter wells,  \Omega \sim 10^{-15}  L,  c \sim 10^{-9}  M, so  \Omega c \sim 10^{9}  molecules per well. The relative noise is  \sim 3 \times 10^{-5} , which is small but non-negligible. For single-molecule regimes, the ODE picture fails entirely and Gillespie SSA is required.

B.6 Spectral Decomposition of the Memory Kernel

Proposition B.6.1 (Memory Kernel Decomposition). Consider a linear reservoir  x_{t+1} = A x_t + C u_t  with diagonalizable  A = V \Lambda V^{-1} , where  \Lambda = \mathrm{diag}(\lambda_1, \ldots, \lambda_N) . The memory kernel for lag  \tau  is:

K(\tau) = C^\top (A^\top)^\tau \Gamma_x^{-1} A^\tau C = \sum_{i=1}^{N} \sum_{j=1}^{N} \alpha_{ij} (\lambda_i \lambda_j)^\tau

where  \alpha_{ij}  are coefficients depending on  V, C,  and  \Gamma_x .

Proof.

Substitute  A = V \Lambda V^{-1} :

A^\tau = V \Lambda^\tau V^{-1} = \sum_{i=1}^{N} \lambda_i^\tau v_i w_i^\top

where  v_i  are right eigenvectors and  w_i  are left eigenvectors (rows of  V^{-1} ). Then:

A^\tau C = \sum_{i=1}^{N} \lambda_i^\tau v_i (w_i^\top C)

and

C^\top (A^\top)^\tau = \sum_{j=1}^{N} \lambda_j^\tau (C^\top v_j) w_j^\top

The product involves terms  (\lambda_i \lambda_j)^\tau . Collecting coefficients yields the stated form. □

Corollary B.6.2 (Slow Modes Dominate Idle Memory). The memory kernel decays as a sum of exponentials with rates  -\log|\lambda_i \lambda_j| . The slowest-decaying modes are those with  |\lambda_i|  closest to 1. For idle memory over long delays, the reservoir must have eigenvalues with  |\lambda_i| \approx 1 , i.e., near-marginal stability.

Corollary B.6.3 (Lyapunov Spectrum Relation). For a nonlinear reservoir linearized around an attractor, the Lyapunov exponents  \mu_i = \log|\lambda_i|  determine the memory decay rates. The memory horizon for mode  i  is  H_i = 1/|\mu_i| . The total memory capacity is dominated by modes with  |\mu_i| \ll 1 .

B.7 Volterra Series Expansion for Nonlinear Reservoirs

Proposition B.7.1 (Volterra Expansion). Consider a nonlinear reservoir with state update  x_{t+1} = F(x_t, u_t)  and linear readout  \hat{y}_t = W^\top x_t . For sufficiently small inputs, the output admits a Volterra series expansion:

\hat{y}_t = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \sum_{\tau_1, \ldots, \tau_n} h_n(\tau_1, \ldots, \tau_n) \prod_{i=1}^{n} u_{t-\tau_i}

where  h_n  are the Volterra kernels of order  n . The linear memory capacity corresponds to the  n=1  term; the information processing capacity includes all orders.

Proof.

Expand  F(x, u)  in a Taylor series around  x = 0, u = 0 :

F(x, u) = F(0, 0) + A x + B u + \sum_{p+q \geq 2} \frac{1}{p! q!} \frac{\partial^{p+q} F}{\partial x^p \partial u^q} x^p u^q

Iterating this expansion and collecting terms by order in  u  yields the Volterra series. The kernels  h_n  are determined by the derivatives of  F  and the readout weights  W . □

Corollary B.7.2 (IPC Bound). The total information processing capacity satisfies:

\mathrm{IPC} = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \sum_{\{\tau_i\}} C_n(\tau_1, \ldots, \tau_n) \leq N

with equality only for linear reservoirs ( h_n = 0  for  n \geq 2 ). Nonlinear reservoirs trade linear capacity for nonlinear capacity.

