Appendix C — Spectral Theory and Lyapunov Analysis

C.1 Derivation of the Memory Capacity Expression

For a linear ESN  x_t = A x_{t-1} + C u_t  with  \rho(A) < 1 , the stationary state is:

x_t = \sum_{j=0}^{\infty} A^j C u_{t-j}

The covariance between  x_t  and  u_{t-\tau}  is:

\mathrm{Cov}(x_t, u_{t-\tau}) = \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{j=0}^{\infty} A^j C u_{t-j} u_{t-\tau} \right] = A^\tau C \sigma_u^2

The variance of the state is:

\Gamma_x = \mathrm{Var}(x_t) = \sigma_u^2 \sum_{j=0}^{\infty} A^j C C^\top (A^j)^\top

The optimal linear reconstruction of  u_{t-\tau}  from  x_t  is:

\hat{u}_{t-\tau} = \frac{\mathrm{Cov}(u_{t-\tau}, x_t)^\top \Gamma_x^{-1} x_t}{\sigma_u^2} u_{t-\tau}

The  \tau -lag memory capacity is the squared correlation:

\mathrm{MC}_\tau = \frac{\mathrm{Cov}(u_{t-\tau}, x_t)^\top \Gamma_x^{-1} \mathrm{Cov}(u_{t-\tau}, x_t)}{\sigma_u^2} = \sigma_u^2 \cdot C^\top (A^\top)^\tau \Gamma_x^{-1} A^\tau C

Summing over all lags:

\mathrm{MC} = \sum_{\tau=0}^{\infty} \mathrm{MC}_\tau = \sigma_u^2 \sum_{\tau=0}^{\infty} C^\top (A^\top)^\tau \Gamma_x^{-1} A^\tau C = \sigma_u^2 \cdot \mathrm{tr}\left( \Gamma_x^{-1} \sum_{\tau=0}^{\infty} A^\tau C C^\top (A^\top)^\tau \right)

Since  \sum_{\tau=0}^{\infty} A^\tau C C^\top (A^\top)^\tau = \Gamma_x / \sigma_u^2 :

\mathrm{MC} = \sigma_u^2 \cdot \mathrm{tr}\left( \Gamma_x^{-1} \cdot \frac{\Gamma_x}{\sigma_u^2} \right) = \mathrm{tr}(I_N) = N

if  \Gamma_x  is invertible. If  \Gamma_x  has rank  r < N , then  \mathrm{MC} = r .

C.2 Derivation of the Decay Model from Spectral Decomposition

From Proposition B.6.1, the memory kernel is:

K(\tau) = \sum_{i,j} \alpha_{ij} (\lambda_i \lambda_j)^\tau

For a reservoir with a single dominant slow mode  \lambda_1 \approx 1  and all other modes fast ( |\lambda_i| \ll 1 ), the kernel is dominated by:

K(\tau) \approx \alpha_{11} |\lambda_1|^{2\tau}

Taking the logarithm:

\log K(\tau) \approx \log \alpha_{11} - 2\tau |\log |\lambda_1||

This is a linear decay in  \tau , corresponding to an exponential decay of mutual information:

I(m; y_\Delta) \approx I_0 e^{-\Delta / \tau_{\mathrm{idle}}}

where  \tau_{\mathrm{idle}} = 1 / (2 |\log |\lambda_1||) .

Remark C.2.1 (Multiple Slow Modes). For systems with multiple slow modes, the decay is a sum of exponentials, and the overall decay may exhibit power-law behavior if the eigenvalue density near  |\lambda| = 1  is continuous.

Remark C.2.2 (Substrate-Specific Decay Families). The exponential model is one of four candidate families:

1. Exponential:  R(\Delta) = I_0 e^{-\Delta/\tau}  — single dominant slow mode.
2. Power-law:  R(\Delta) = I_0 \Delta^{-\alpha}  — continuous eigenvalue density (iontronic).
3. Step-function collapse:  R(\Delta) = I_0 \Theta(\Delta_c - \Delta)  — metastable basin (BZ-AOT).
4. Oscillatory:  R(\Delta) = I_0 e^{-\Delta/\tau} \cos(\omega \Delta + \phi)  — phase-carrying modes.

Phase Ic must fit all four families non-parametrically and report the best-fitting model per substrate.

C.3 Benettin Algorithm for Lyapunov Spectrum

Algorithm (Benettin / Shimada-Nagashima). Given the reservoir map  F: \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{U} \to \mathcal{X}  and a reference trajectory  \{x_t\} , the Lyapunov spectrum  \{\mu_1, \ldots, \mu_N\}  is computed as follows:

Initialize:

· Orthonormal tangent vectors  \{v_1^{(0)}, \ldots, v_N^{(0)}\} \subset \mathbb{R}^N .
· Running sums  S_i = 0  for  i = 1, \ldots, N .
· Number of iterations  T .

For  t = 1, 2, \ldots, T :

1. Integrate the trajectory:  x_t = F(x_{t-1}, u_t) .
2. Compute the Jacobian:  J_t = D_x F(x_{t-1}, u_t) .
3. Propagate tangent vectors:  w_i = J_t \cdot v_i^{(t-1)} .
4. Gram-Schmidt orthonormalization:
   ·  u_1 = w_1 / \|w_1\| 
   ·  u_2 = (w_2 - (w_2 \cdot u_1) u_1) / \|w_2 - (w_2 \cdot u_1) u_1\| 
   ·  u_3 = (w_3 - (w_3 \cdot u_1) u_1 - (w_3 \cdot u_2) u_2) / \|\cdots\| 
   ·  \ldots 
5. Accumulate log norms:  S_i \mathrel{+}= \log \|w_i^{\perp}\| .
6. Reset:  v_i^{(t)} = u_i .

Output:

\mu_i = \frac{S_i}{T} \quad \text{for } i = 1, \ldots, N

Interpretation for CEMI. The Lyapunov spectrum partitions into:

· Memory-carrying modes:  \mu_i \approx 0^-  (slow decay, long memory horizon).
· ESP-enforcing modes:  \mu_i \ll 0  (fast contraction, wash-out of initial conditions).

The design target for idle memory is:

·  \mu_1 < 0  with  |\mu_1|  small (near-marginal stability).
· Many modes with  |\mu_i| \ll 1  (many slow modes).
· No modes with  \mu_i > 0  (no chaos).

