D.2 Upper-Bound Estimator for Idle Capacity

For each independent sensor channel  i , the data-processing inequality and the sensor-noise model give:

C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{idle}}(\Delta) \leq \sum_{i=1}^{k} \frac{1}{2} \log\left(1 + \frac{\mathrm{Var}(s_i)}{\mathrm{Var}(\eta_i)}\right)

where  s_i  is the signal at  t_0 + \Delta  and  \eta_i  is the sensor noise on channel  i . This is computable from published sensor specifications without new experiments.

Justification. For a Gaussian channel with signal variance  \sigma_s^2  and noise variance  \sigma_\eta^2 , the capacity is  \frac{1}{2} \log(1 + \sigma_s^2/\sigma_\eta^2) . Summing over independent channels gives the stated bound. The bound is an upper bound because it assumes optimal encoding and decoding.

D.3 Channel Capacity of a Well-Mixed CRN

For a well-mixed CRN with mass-action kinetics, the steady-state concentrations are a smooth function of the input flow rates. The channel capacity is bounded by the rank of the Jacobian of the steady-state map:

C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{idle}} \leq \mathrm{rank}\left(\frac{\partial x_{\mathrm{ss}}}{\partial u}\right) \cdot \frac{1}{2} \log\left(1 + \mathrm{SNR}\right)

where  x_{\mathrm{ss}}  is the steady state and  u  is the input. This bound is computable from the ODE model.

