Appendix F — Volterra Series and IPC Decomposition

F.1 Volterra Kernel Derivation

For a nonlinear reservoir with state update  x_{t+1} = F(x_t, u_t) , expand  F  in a Taylor series:

F(x, u) = F(0, 0) + A x + B u + \frac{1}{2} \left[ x^\top H_{xx} x + 2 x^\top H_{xu} u + u^\top H_{uu} u \right] + O(\|(x, u)\|^3)

where  A = \partial F/\partial x ,  B = \partial F/\partial u ,  H_{xx} = \partial^2 F/\partial x^2 , etc.

Iterating this expansion over time and collecting terms by order in  u :

\hat{y}_t = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \sum_{\tau_1, \ldots, \tau_n} h_n(\tau_1, \ldots, \tau_n) \prod_{i=1}^{n} u_{t-\tau_i}

The first-order kernel is:

h_1(\tau) = W^\top A^\tau B

The second-order kernel is:

h_2(\tau_1, \tau_2) = W^\top A^{\tau_1} H_{xu} A^{\tau_2} B + \text{symmetric terms}

F.2 IPC Decomposition

The information processing capacity decomposes as:

\mathrm{IPC} = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \mathrm{IPC}_n

where  \mathrm{IPC}_n  is the capacity for  n -th order Volterra functionals. For a linear reservoir,  \mathrm{IPC}_n = 0  for  n \geq 2 , and  \mathrm{IPC} = \mathrm{MC} \leq N . For a nonlinear reservoir, capacity is distributed across orders, with the constraint:

\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \mathrm{IPC}_n \leq N

F.3 Chemical Interpretation of Volterra Kernels

Volterra order Chemical interpretation
 h_1  First-order reaction kinetics (mass action)
 h_2  Second-order reactions (bimolecular)
 h_3  Autocatalysis, trimolecular
 h_n   n -body reactions

The RNCRN can be trained to shape these kernels by designing the reaction network topology and rate constants.

