Appendix G — Substrate-Specific Calculations

G.1 BZ Array Capacity Estimate

The BZ array has 25 cells, each with 2 states (on/off), giving  2^{25} = 3.3 \times 10^7  addressable states. The proposal cites  2.9 \times 10^{17}  chemical states, which accounts for additional degrees of freedom per cell (amplitude, phase, etc.).

Addressable bits:  \log_2(2.9 \times 10^{17}) \approx 58  bits.

Usable bits after discounting: If observation bandwidth allows discrimination of  K  levels per cell, the usable state count is  K^{25} . For  K = 4 :

N_{\mathrm{usable}} = 4^{25} = 2^{50} \approx 1.1 \times 10^{15}

so  \log_2 N_{\mathrm{usable}} = 50  bits.

After discounting for noise, drift, and partial observability (assume 50% loss):

C_{\mathrm{eff}} \approx 25 \text{ bits}

Upper bound via SNR: For a single cell with signal amplitude  A  and noise  \sigma , the per-cell capacity is  \frac{1}{2} \log(1 + A^2/\sigma^2) . For  A/\sigma = 10 , this is  \frac{1}{2} \log(101) \approx 2.3  bits. Summing over 25 cells:  C_{\mathrm{eff}} \leq 57.5  bits. Discounting for drift and partial observability gives a realistic estimate of 20–30 bits.

G.2 Formose CSTR Capacity Estimate

The formose reservoir has 106 ion channels at 500 ms resolution. The published forecast horizon is 120 s. The mutual information between input and ion signal as a function of lag is available in the published work.

Driven capacity: The published data suggest  I(u; y_\Delta)  decays over 120 s. Extrapolating to  \Delta = 1  hour is not justified without idle-regime data.

Idle capacity: No published data. Phase 0 must estimate upper bounds from physical constraints (residence time, diffusion, reaction kinetics). A conservative upper bound is  C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{idle}} \leq 0  if flow stops (concentrations frozen, no dynamics).

G.3 DNA RNCRN Capacity Estimate

For a DNA RNCRN with  N  species and  R  reactions, the capacity is bounded by:

C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{idle}} \leq \min(N, R) \cdot \frac{1}{2} \log(1 + \mathrm{SNR})

For  N = 100  species,  R = 500  reactions, and  \mathrm{SNR} = 100 :

C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{idle}} \leq 100 \cdot \frac{1}{2} \log(101) \approx 230 \text{ nats}

After discounting for copy-number noise, drift, and partial observability (assume 90% loss):

C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{idle}} \approx 23 \text{ nats}

This corresponds to  M \leq e^{24.3} \approx 3.6 \times 10^{10}  distinguishable states at 5% error. This is a far more optimistic estimate than BZ or formose, supporting the prioritization of DNA as the primary substrate.

G.4 Iontronic Capacity Estimate

For an iontronic device with power-law relaxation  K(t) \sim t^{-\alpha} , the capacity is:

C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{idle}} \leq \frac{1}{2} \log\left(1 + \frac{\sigma_s^2}{\sigma_\eta^2}\right)

For  \sigma_s/\sigma_\eta = 10  and  \alpha = 0.5 :

C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{idle}} \approx 2.3 \text{ bits per channel}

Summing over 10 independent channels gives  C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{idle}} \approx 23  bits.

