Appendix H — Experimental Protocols

H.1 Phase 0: Capacity Screening Protocol

Step 0.1 — Lyapunov spectrum. For each candidate, integrate the ODE model (Oregonator for BZ, Dack et al. 2026 RNCRN for DNA) and apply the Benettin algorithm. Report  \{\mu_i\}  and the number of modes with  |\mu_i| < 0.01 .

Step 0.2 — ESP margin. Compute  L = \sup_x \|Df(x)\|  numerically by sampling the state space.

Step 0.3 — Driven capacity. Extract  C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{driven}}(\Delta)  from published data (Baltussen 2024 for formose, Parrilla-Gutierrez 2020 for BZ).

Step 0.4 — Idle capacity upper bound. Compute  C_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{idle}}(\Delta) \leq \sum_i \frac{1}{2} \log(1 + \mathrm{SNR}_i)  from sensor specifications.

Step 0.5 — Decay-model fit. For each substrate, fit the four candidate families (exponential, power-law, step-function, oscillatory) to the empirical  R(\Delta)  where available.

Step 0.6 — Negative table. Publish the results in a Markdown + CSV format in the CEMI repository.

H.2 Phase Ia: WRITE Protocol Learning

Training loop:

1. Initialize encoder  E_\phi  and decoder  D_\psi  with random weights.
2. Sample memory  m \sim \mathrm{Uniform}(\mathcal{M}) .
3. Encode:  u_{t_0:t_0+T} = E_\phi(m, q, x_{t_0}) .
4. Simulate:  x_{1:T} = F(u_{1:T})  (RNCRN forward pass).
5. Sense:  y = H(x_T) + \eta .
6. Decode:  \hat{m} = D_\psi(y) .
7. Loss:  \mathcal{L} = \|m - \hat{m}\|^2 + \lambda \Omega(\phi, \psi) .
8. Gradient:  \nabla_\phi \mathcal{L}, \nabla_\psi \mathcal{L}  via backprop through simulator.
9. Update:  \phi \leftarrow \phi - \alpha \nabla_\phi \mathcal{L}; \psi \leftarrow \psi - \alpha \nabla_\psi \mathcal{L} .

Backpropagation through RNCRN: Use the adjoint method. Forward:  dx/dt = f(x, u, \theta) . Adjoint:  da/dt = -a^\top \partial f/\partial x . Gradient:  d\mathcal{L}/d\theta = \int a^\top \partial f/\partial \theta \, dt . Memory cost:  O(1)  with checkpointing, else  O(T) .

H.3 Phase Ib: READ Protocol Learning

Setup: The RNCRN dynamics  F  are hidden from the agent. The agent only sees observation  y_t  and reward  R_t .

POMDP environment:

· Agent: policy  \pi_\theta(a \mid y_{1:t})  (LSTM or Transformer).
· Environment: chemical reservoir  F , observation  y_t = H(x_t) + \eta .
· Reward:  R_t = -\|\hat{m}_t - m_t\|^2 .

Algorithms: PPO, SAC, evolution strategies, Bayesian optimization.

Comparison: Oracle (Phase Ia) vs. black-box (Phase Ib). Metric: recall accuracy ratio. Threshold: ratio > 0.8 for H3 acceptance.

H.4 Phase Ic: Idle Decay Protocol

1. Write: Apply input sequence  u_{1:T}  encoding memory  m .
2. Quench: Stop all forcing (flow = 0, light = off). Record time  t_0 .
3. Wait: For delays  \Delta \in \{1 \text{ min}, 5 \text{ min}, 15 \text{ min}, 30 \text{ min}, 1 \text{ hr}, 2 \text{ hr}, 4 \text{ hr}\} , record observation  y_{t_0 + \Delta} .
4. Read: Apply decoder  D_\psi  to  y_{t_0 + \Delta}  to obtain  \hat{m} .
5. Estimate: Compute  I(m; \hat{m})  using the empirical distribution over many trials.
6. Fit: Compare decay models (exponential, power-law, step, oscillatory).

Controls: Measure system drift with no memory written (baseline noise). Measure sensor noise with a known state.

H.5 Phase II: Black-Box Interface Hardening

Introduce drift, sensor noise  \eta_t , forgetting, degradation, and state convergence. Test whether an agent can learn refreshing, redundancy, robust encoding, and robust retrieval.

Null models:

· N1 (Shuffled kinetics): Permute reaction rate constants  \{k_j\}  across reactions, preserving marginal distribution.
· N2 (Linearized kinetics): Replace mass-action rates with first-order rates matched to the Jacobian at the attractor.
· N3 (Random stoichiometry): Replace  S  with a random matrix matched in rank and spectral norm.

H.6 Phase III: In Chemico Validation

Only after successful numerical validation should the learned interface be transferred to a physical microfluidic chemical reservoir. A suitable platform would be based on existing chemical reservoir computing architectures — the formose CSTR reservoir or a BZ array. The iontronic route (nanofluidic memristors with history-dependent conductance) offers a lower-energy alternative.

EHS requirements:

· BZ: Fume hood, bromate waste segregation.
· Formose: Formaldehyde scrubber, closed-loop flow.
· DNA: Institutional biohazard policy for oligonucleotide waste.

H.7 Phase IV: Demonstration of Advantage

Only if earlier hypotheses succeed should the research ask whether chemical dynamics provide a distinctive memory property.

